from pathlib import Path
from bs4 import BeautifulSoup

root=Path(__file__).resolve().parent
sections=[
 ('The editorial perspective','Taler Vox introduces J. M. Barrie, the cultural setting of Peter Pan, its main characters, and the threads that connect the Darling children’s journey with Peter’s refusal to grow up. The perspective proposes childhood, family, time, and loss as ways into the story. Barrie’s seventeen chapters then let readers encounter those ideas in the narrator’s own playful, sometimes unsettling voice.'),
 ('I. Peter Breaks Through','Wendy Darling first learns that children grow up. Her parents try to manage the costs and anxieties of family life, while Nana the dog guards the nursery. Mrs. Darling notices Peter Pan in the children’s minds and later sees him at the window. The chapter places the impossible beside ordinary domestic worries before Peter enters the house.'),
 ('II. The Shadow','Peter returns to the nursery for his lost shadow, accompanied by Tinker Bell. Wendy wakes, talks to him, and sews the shadow back on. Peter’s pride, forgetfulness, and stories about the Lost Boys fascinate her. He needs someone to tell them stories, so he invites Wendy to Neverland; John and Michael are soon drawn into the invitation.'),
 ('III. Come Away, Come Away!','The children learn to fly with fairy dust and thoughts that lift them, then leave through the window. Their parents and Nana come back too late to stop them. The departure is exhilarating, yet the narrator keeps the loss felt at home in view. Peter promises adventure while showing how little he understands the ties the children are leaving.'),
 ('IV. The Flight','The journey through the night is long and dangerous. Peter darts away, returns, and sometimes forgets his companions; Wendy tries to look after her brothers. As Neverland appears, pirate threats and Tinker Bell’s jealousy turn the flight from a game into a risk. The children arrive in a place shaped partly by their own imaginations.'),
 ('V. The Island Come True','The island’s groups move toward one another: Lost Boys, pirates, and other inhabitants respond to the arrivals. Tinker Bell misleads the boys, and Wendy is struck by an arrow before Peter reaches her. The mistake reveals how quickly make-believe orders can hurt someone, and how much the boys want Wendy to survive.'),
 ('VI. The Little House','The Lost Boys build a small house around Wendy as she recovers. Peter takes pride in it, and the children begin to play at having a home. Wendy’s role as mother offers warmth, but the game also asks her to take responsibility for others while Peter remains free to come and go.'),
 ('VII. The Home Under the Ground','The children settle into an underground home, with meals, stories, and routines that echo the Darling household. Peter leads adventures, while Wendy tries to keep the boys together and remembers her parents. Neverland gives them freedom from adult rules, yet their invented family cannot fully replace the one left behind.'),
 ('VIII. The Mermaids’ Lagoon','An outing to the lagoon becomes dangerous when pirates capture Tiger Lily. Peter uses imitation and quick thinking to help her, then faces Captain Hook. The struggle on the rock exposes Peter’s courage and Hook’s resentment, while Wendy sees that the island’s beauty offers little protection from violence.'),
 ('IX. The Never Bird','After the danger at the lagoon, Peter is stranded as the tide rises. A Never Bird brings her nest near him; he uses it to float toward safety and helps protect her eggs. The brief rescue balances daring with care, and gives Peter a rare moment of attention to another creature’s needs.'),
 ('X. The Happy Home','Back underground, the children’s domestic play continues. Wendy tells stories, Peter takes the father’s place only uneasily, and the Lost Boys ask what family means. Peter can perform a role, but resists the lasting feeling and obligation behind it. The home is both a refuge and a reminder of what the children miss.'),
 ('XI. Wendy’s Story','Wendy tells the boys about children who flew away and about a mother who kept the nursery window open for their return. As she speaks, John and Michael begin to fear being forgotten, and the Lost Boys want to go with them. Peter refuses to leave Neverland, while Wendy’s story turns longing for home into a decision.'),
 ('XII. The Children Are Carried Off','As Wendy and the children prepare to leave, pirates ambush them. Hook’s crew carries them away, while Peter is left behind. The play of being pirates becomes a real threat to the children, and the separation tests whether Peter will act for others rather than only for another adventure.'),
 ('XIII. Do You Believe in Fairies?','Hook leaves poison for Peter, but Tinker Bell drinks it to save him. Peter appeals for belief in fairies, and her life depends on the answer. The scene gives the story’s theatrical magic an urgent purpose: Peter’s companion, so often jealous and impulsive, has made a selfless choice.'),
 ('XIV. The Pirate Ship','The children face Hook and his crew aboard the ship. Peter reaches the vessel and secretly changes the balance of power. The pirates’ fear of the ticking crocodile sharpens the tension, while Wendy and the others wait for a chance to escape. Peter’s arrival turns the rescue into a confrontation.'),
 ('XV. Hook or Me This Time','Peter and Hook fight on the ship. Hook’s concern with manners and reputation contrasts with Peter’s audacity, but neither is a simple model of maturity. When Peter prevails, the children can sail away. The victory ends the immediate danger without settling the larger question of whether Peter can belong to a family.'),
 ('XVI. The Return Home','Wendy, John, and Michael return to the Darling nursery, where their parents have continued to grieve and hope. The Lost Boys are welcomed into the household as well. Peter looks in from outside and declines to stay. The reunion gives the children a future of growth, while Peter chooses the recurring freedom of Neverland.'),
 ('XVII. When Wendy Grew Up','Years pass. Wendy becomes an adult and has a daughter, Jane, who loves hearing the old story. Peter returns almost unchanged and discovers that Wendy can no longer fly. Jane goes with him instead, and later another generation follows. Barrie ends with a cycle of childhood imagination and an ache for the time Peter cannot share with those who grow up.'),
]
out=BeautifulSoup('<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>Peter Pan — Ten-minute read</title></head><body><article data-reader-content></article></body></html>','html.parser')
h=out.new_tag('h1',id='book-title');h.string='Peter Pan';out.article.append(h)
for i,(title,text) in enumerate(sections,1):
 h=out.new_tag('h2',id=f'short-{i}');h.string=title;out.article.append(h)
 p=out.new_tag('p');p.string=text;out.article.append(p)
target=root/'book/en/short_content.html';target.write_text(str(out)+'\n')
print('Prepared short:',len(out.article.get_text(' ',strip=True).split()),'words',len(sections),'sections')
